# conversations-small-talk.kb - Conversations / Small talk
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 6 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.

compliment | definition | A compliment praises something about a person, and it works best when it is specific, because a general one lands as an attempt to extract something. Saying you look well is heard as an opening, while saying the new haircut suits you is heard as a compliment. The rule that catches people is not to attach a request immediately, because the two together turn the praise into a transaction and the other person can hear it happen. | compliment, praise, specific, general, request, transaction | how to give a compliment, why is you look well not a compliment, compliment and asking together | compliment |Compliment
common ground | definition | Common ground is what two people already agree on, and conversation runs on it, which is why finding some is the first job when talking to a stranger. It is usually found in shared circumstance rather than shared opinion, so being in the same queue helps more than discovering you both like the same band. Shared opinions work less well, because agreeing too quickly reads as flattery and disagreeing too quickly ends the exchange. | common ground, agree, stranger, opinion, flattery, exchange | what is common ground, how to find common ground, is shared opinion good for small talk | common ground | magkakatugma
current events | definition | Current events are the things happening now, and they are a reliable small-talk subject for about a week and then a trap. The turn comes when a story develops into something divisive, so a subject that opened a conversation can shut it a fortnight later. The safe version is something with a factual answer and no stake in it, which is why weather and sport outlast politics in nearly every culture ever surveyed. | current events, now, politics, trap, safe, survey | is current events good small talk, what is current events, why politics is bad small talk | current events | balita
safe topic | definition | A safe topic is one that cannot be read as a challenge, and the reliable examples are the surroundings, the occasion and the food. What makes them safe is not that they are interesting but that everybody already has an answer and nobody is being asked for a view. The unseemly part is how often people use them to dodge a question they were hoping to be asked, and a host who steers every talk to the weather is doing exactly that. | safe topic, challenge, surroundings, occasion, food, host | what is a safe small talk topic, best safe topics, how to avoid a subject | safe topic | ligtas na paksa
open question | definition | An open question cannot be answered in one word, and that is its entire function, since a closed question ends a turn while an open one extends it. What did you make of the film invites several sentences; did you like the film invites yes or no. The trade-off is that open questions need somebody willing to answer at length, so in a group they often go unanswered while the closed ones get answered instead. | open question, answer, extend, turn, closed, group | what is an open question, open vs closed questions, how to ask open questions | open question | bukas na tanong
chat | definition | To chat is to talk without an agenda, and the word implies a low-stakes conversation where the point is the company rather than an outcome. It is also a countable noun in British usage, where have a chat means a friendly talk, and that survives in a few fixed phrases. The difference from a discussion is the absence of a conclusion, and the difference from small talk is that a chat can go on for as long as the people want. | chat, agenda, company, discussion, conclusion, friendly | what does chat mean, have a chat meaning, difference between chat and discussion | chat | usap
